### Rationale
The hand-written tcgen05 TF32 kernel worked first time. Its weight (W) loads carry an L2 evict_first hint, so they do not push the harness's dirty L2 lines out during the timed run. On the rented B200 it passed 16/16 and runs 42.0–45.7 µs at every B ≤ 128, against 48.8–55.8 µs for r1 on the same GPU. At B = 131/192/211 it runs 44.1/49.3/50.6 µs, against 56.9/56.2/60.5. That is −17% in the S band, and the emulator predicts 0.627 against r1's portal 0.588. In one interleaved probe the hint alone saved 6–9 µs. Each pipeline stage now holds 4 (or 2–3) k-blocks of 32 instead of one, which saved another 2–4 µs.

Max error against fp64 stays at 1.27–1.62e-3, but only because the TMA tensor maps use the TFLOAT32 data type (the error suggests TMA rounds on load). The FLOAT32 type gives 3.6–4.2e-3, which is over the 2e-3 atol.

Two targets are just missed: B = 128 runs 45.7 µs (target ≤ 45) and B = 211 runs 50.6 µs (target ≤ 50). Step 1, patching the CUTLASS mainloop, was skipped because the hand-written kernel tested the same mechanism directly. B > 256 still runs the r1 CUTLASS tiles.

